# CS 250 – Sprint Review & Retrospective

This repository contains my Sprint Review and Retrospective deliverable for the SNHU Travel project. Throughout the course, I applied Agile and Scrum practices including user story creation, backlog refinement, sprint planning, daily standups, and iterative development.

---

## Sprint Review & Retrospective

### Applying Roles

Throughout the SNHU Travel development project, each Scrum role contributed directly to the team’s ability to deliver working increments of software. Because I rotated roles each week, I gained firsthand experience with how each role supports the overall success of an Agile team.

As the Product Owner, I defined and prioritized user stories based on business value. For example, I created the user story, “As a traveler, I want to search for destinations so that I can plan my trip,” and clarified acceptance criteria so the development team could begin work without ambiguity. This role ensured that the team always had a clear vision and a prioritized backlog aligned with stakeholder needs.

As the Scrum Master, I facilitated sprint events, encouraged adherence to Agile principles, and removed impediments. When requirements shifted mid‑sprint, I guided the team through re‑evaluating the sprint goal and adjusting the scope. This helped maintain momentum and prevented disruptions from derailing progress.

As a Developer, I contributed directly to building features for the SNHU Travel application. I broke down user stories into actionable tasks, estimated effort, and collaborated during daily standups. For example, when developing the destination search interface, I communicated early that the data structure conflicted with the UI layout. This transparency prevented rework and allowed the team to resolve the issue quickly.

Together, these roles created a cohesive environment where direction, facilitation, and execution aligned to support successful delivery.

---

### Completing User Stories

The Scrum‑Agile approach significantly improved the completion of user stories by emphasizing iterative development, continuous feedback, and small, manageable increments of work. User stories were written from the traveler’s perspective, which kept the team focused on delivering user value.

For example, the user story related to displaying travel packages was completed efficiently because the team broke it into smaller tasks: designing the interface, connecting the data, and testing functionality. Daily standups allowed us to identify blockers early. When I noticed that the UI layout did not match the data schema, I raised the issue during standup, and the team collaborated to adjust the schema before development continued.

Scrum’s iterative nature also supported early validation. At the end of each sprint, we delivered a working increment of the application, which allowed stakeholders to provide feedback immediately. This prevented late‑stage surprises and ensured that the product evolved in alignment with user expectations.

---

### Handling Interruptions

One of the strongest advantages of Scrum was its ability to handle interruptions and changes in direction. Midway through development, SNHU Travel requested a modification to the trip‑planning workflow to make it more flexible and user‑friendly.

In a waterfall model, this change would have required revisiting documentation, re‑approving requirements, and potentially delaying the entire project. In contrast, Scrum allowed us to adapt quickly. We added the new requirement to the product backlog, re‑prioritized items, and adjusted the sprint scope during sprint planning.

This flexibility ensured that the team continued delivering value without losing progress. The ability to incorporate change efficiently is a core benefit of Agile methodologies.

---

### Communication

Effective communication was essential throughout the project. Below are examples of communication that supported collaboration and clarity:

Daily Standup Example: “Yesterday I completed the destination search UI. Today I’m connecting to the API. I’m blocked because the endpoint documentation is unclear.” This communication was effective because it followed the standup structure and immediately highlighted a blocker the team could address.

Backlog Clarification Example (as Product Owner): “Let’s update the acceptance criteria for the travel package story to include filtering by price and region.” This encouraged collaboration by ensuring developers had clarity before beginning work.

Sprint Review Example: “Here is the working demo of the trip planner. We completed three user stories and received feedback that will be added to the next sprint backlog.” This communication was effective because it reinforced transparency and kept stakeholders informed.

These examples demonstrate how structured, concise communication supports alignment and teamwork in a Scrum environment.

