# Module Eight Journal Reflection

### How do I interpret user needs and implement them into a program? How does creating “user stories” help with this?

I interpret user needs by focusing on what users want to accomplish and why it matters. User stories help translate those needs into clear, actionable requirements. The “As a user, I want… so that…” format keeps development centered on delivering real value and ensures acceptance criteria are clear.

### How do I approach developing programs? What Agile processes do I hope to incorporate into my future development work?

I prefer an iterative development approach—building small pieces, testing, and improving through feedback. Agile processes like sprint planning, daily standups, backlog refinement, and retrospectives help maintain structure while allowing flexibility. I plan to use these practices in future development work to stay aligned with user needs and reduce risk.

### What does it mean to be a good team member in software development?

A good team member communicates clearly, collaborates respectfully, and supports shared goals. It means being reliable, adaptable, and willing to help others. Good teamwork also involves giving and receiving feedback constructively and keeping user needs at the center of decisions.
